kant.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: kant.logic has "holds → promise_possible"; kant.logic.2 and kant.logic.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "the maxim holds as a universal law of nature without contradicting itself" is true, which the second allows
kant.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered yes, which keeps kant.logic.2 and kant.logic.3, to: Suppose "the maxim holds as a universal law of nature without contradicting itself" is true and "the promise itself, and the end one has in view in it, is possible" is false. Is S4, "Suppose however that he resolves to do so: then the maxim of his action would be expressed thus: "When I think myself in want of money, I will borrow money and promise to repay it, although I know that I never can do so."", true then?
kant.logic: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: kant.logic has "holds → universal"; kant.logic.2 and kant.logic.3 have "holds → universal ∧ promise_possible"; they differ when only "my maxim is a universal law: everyone in difficulty may promise whatever he pleases with the purpose of not keeping it" and "the maxim holds as a universal law of nature without contradicting itself" are true, which the first allows
